ence/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/7db943c53e70d9b77490c2d73acac939be08fdfa4b0aa958addb2d50b6078543.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$\n\n$t\\in [$\n$-\\pi ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/e03130cdcae3fff94036453883eb638371b62a4ee26b9b550192aed32f95a39d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ }\\cup \\text{ }(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-325980/field-1" "q-325980/field-2" "q-325980/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325980"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-89350/selection/348a8b4a7fcf2d1c58c5b8fab2d0a6884e7c60f9ed36a3d77e436e84fd15dc7e" :answer/id #uuid "e8a3d873-6d68-5ff9-9dcd-15455ae73d4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89350/selection/b85ba2ba54d596653a563476b4f78775ae06d7693e7e2bfe7b1bde461b8c1155" :answer/id #uuid "057fdd73-dd6b-563e-bc50-f9bcfa1485ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math