arameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/d8f735790daf267a094ecd8c336d90de65d84e3985485213eb541364bd14aacd.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-106598/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106598"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325985/field-1/93b23be7eccffafc428741fe46d5e1a8a45d9a44730ec29ee6c035137bd18fed" :answer/id #uuid "8e79ab75-6bf0-51df-b198-33b5f76831cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325985/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "14140dcb-a619-5447-bdc3-acc8ad253aad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325985/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "feb3c842-a366-5fa5-b0eb-b3e573c27efb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325985/field-1/93b23be7eccffafc428741fe46d5e1a8a45d9a44730ec29ee6c035137bd18fed" :answer-field/choices ["q-325985/field-1/93b23be7eccffafc428741fe46d5e1a8a45d9a44730ec29ee6c035137bd18fed" "q-325985/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325985/field-2/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer/id #uuid "87825b4c-9345-5473-8ba0-aee302e06a27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325985/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "173700aa-587b-5075-ba28-7abe8c0039d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325985/field-2/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-325985/field-2/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325985" :question/id #uuid "9f76d65f-5129-5d16-94bc-f52d8a4e4d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325985" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/5b133ac885861d82704a6d6c2d99b8e4179210e4f28e94bc6d7283da23f97042.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this quarter, assuming that the entire parabola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6ac31692404208d80637291fb30be028a7efdc3a172af0337da26039f1e87326.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-325985/field-1" "q-325985/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325985"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-89335/selection/11ea745910ff697b9055ddbb7e7b8a23d1d92eec0a8c07053d7df7aa989d1cf5" :answer/id #uuid "9d974207-11f7-5c26-ae8b-7c3ae8870574" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89335/selection/b85ba2ba54d596653a563476b4f78775ae06d7693e7e2bfe7b1bde461b8c1155" :answer/id #uuid "2ec629d0-52d3-505a-bcb9-b65cb5d919f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89335/selection/7815766c3d40aa5830f35a4589f31a583d578ff12d207e41785c4ae03823a742" :answer/id #uuid "beda32a1-86fb-50fd-aea7-31a07b6249b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,0],\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89335/selection/ca46cfd576f36f8ab6b5aa55b08297e97224a500c0d3c29fe92d97ff7d182a11" :answer/id #uuid "6cfc2058-4cd0-5506-8a29-93e54a1eb58b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,t\\ne 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89335/selection/329e61757dd4319d7a3c4ef4eafb81646fef59b1a90b5167f37b3260a1d445cb" :answer/id #uuid "8c0145f8-1c7b-57bc-bf7f-57887b782f47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89335/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9dfa021-a27c-5bbf-ad11-0eaca5b95334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-89335/selection/b85ba2ba54d596653a563476b4f78775ae06d7693e7e2bfe7b1bde461b8c1155" :answer-field/choices ["q-89335/selection/11ea745910ff697b9055ddbb7e7b8a23d1d92eec0a8c07053d7df7aa989d1cf5" "q-89335/selection/b85ba2ba54d596653a563476b4f78775ae06d7693e7e2bfe7b1bde461b8c1155" "q-89335/selection/7815766c3d40aa5830f35a4589f31a583d578ff12d207e41785c4ae03823a742" "q-89335/selection/ca46cfd576f36f8ab6b5aa55b08297e97224a500c0d3c29fe92d97ff7d182a11" "q-89335/selection/329e61757dd4319d7a3c4ef4eafb81646fef59b1a90b5167f37b3260a1d445cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-89335" :question/id #uuid "f5decbaf-577e-5c3b-892a-50907a2e597c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89335" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/23610b57f6867c18f11b0b334bb4902ab92f4b111a2764aa35b5a3a1cec77e2f.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/d91eb69ad250ab786426c2b46d5404bdf8aa43d6c97c1d276be444b678be4ced.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-89335/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89335"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7883"] :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\displaystyle\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{49} = 7$ and $b =\\sqrt9= 3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=3\\tan{t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5378"] :question/problem "Given that the asymptotes of the hyperbola below are $y=\\pm\\dfrac{5}{7}x,$ find the parametric equations of the hyperbola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/fd2618e961c15a9b0665e73e18544ab08b0d611b962bc3771558c61450cf49e5.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a, 0).$ From the graph, we have that $(7,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm\\dfrac57x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{b}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=5\\tan{t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5379"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7896"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6e12d114aedfd6c5c8ab5830e052fcc1dd3c937b825665a0490b42ca0c87972c.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/730325137cfca58eb17d65103e539d084f81d7ab810628261125c29dbb8a0284.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
